    92	## 1. Row normal form: the degree-(8,4) one-place family
    93	
    94	Throughout, a *(8,4) row member* is a polynomial map `t ↦ (P(t),Q(t))`, birational onto
    95	its image `D' ⊂ A²`, with `deg P = 8`, `deg Q = 4`, δ-sequence `Δ=(8,4,6,3)`, and affine
    96	singularities exactly three ordinary nodes. `C' := D̄' ⊂ P²`, `L_∞ = \{Z=0\}`.
    97	Scaling `x,y` makes `P,Q` monic; that is assumed from here.
    98	
    99	### 1.1 The gcd/delta tower and the characteristic data
   100	
   101	`Δ=(8,4,6,3)` is a legitimate Abhyankar–Moh δ-sequence: `d_0=8`, `d_1=4`, `d_2=2`,
   102	`d_3=1`, quotients `n_1=n_2=n_3=2` (all `>1`); `n_1δ_1=8∈⟨8⟩`, `n_2δ_2=12∈⟨8,4⟩`,
   103	`n_3δ_3=6∈⟨8,4,6⟩`; and `δ_2=6<n_1δ_1=8`, `δ_3=3<n_2δ_2=12`. The degree semigroup is
   104	`Γ = ⟨8,4,6,3⟩ = ⟨3,4⟩`, whose gaps are `\{1,2,5\}`; three gaps, matching `δ_aff=3`.
   105	
   106	**The germ at infinity, re-derived.** Homogenise `[X:Y:Z]=[P(t):Q(t):1]` and put
   107	`s=1/t`, `\tilde P := s⁸P`, `\tilde Q := s⁴Q` (both units at `s=0`). In the chart `X=1`,
   108	with `v=Y/X`, `w=Z/X`,
   109	
   110	```text
   111	   v(s) = s⁴·\tilde Q/\tilde P ,      w(s) = s⁸/\tilde P .                     (1.1)
   112	```
   113	
   114	So there is **one place**, at `Q_∞=[1:0:0]`, with `mult_{Q_∞}C' = min(4,8) = 4` and
   115	`I(C',L_∞;Q_∞) = ord_s w = 8`. Bézout `8·1=8` is exhausted at `Q_∞`, so
   116	`C'∩L_∞=\{Q_∞\}` and `L_∞` is the **tangent line** (contact `8 > 4`).
   117	
   118	Both `ord v=4` and `ord w=8` lie in `4ℤ`, so the first genuinely new semigroup element
   119	comes from a cancellation. Since `w - cv² = (s⁸/\tilde P²)(\tilde P - c\tilde Q²)` and
   120	`\tilde P - c\tilde Q² = (1-c)+O(s)`, the only useful constant is `c=1`. Writing
   121	`m := deg_t(P-Q²)`, we have `P-Q² = s^{-m}(\text{unit})`, hence
   122	`\tilde P-\tilde Q² = s^{8-m}(\text{unit})` and
   123	
   124	```text
   125	   ord_s(w - v²) = 16 - m .                                                     (1.2)
   126	```
   127	
   128	Every element of `O_{Q_∞}` is a polynomial in `v` and `u_2 := w-v²`, whose orders are
   129	`4` and `16-m`; so `β̄_1 = 16-m` is the first semigroup generator outside `⟨4⟩`.
   130	
   131	Independently, `δ_2 = \min\{\deg(P-φ(Q)) : \deg φ ≤ 2\}`; subtracting `c_1Q+c_0` only
   132	touches `t`-degrees `4` and `0`, so `δ_2 = m` whenever `m ≥ 5`, and `δ_2 ≤ 4` otherwise.
   133	Therefore, on this row,
   134	
   135	```text
   136	   δ_2 = 6  ⟺  m = deg(P-Q²) = 6  ⟺  β̄_1 = 10 .                              (1.3)
   137	```
   138	
   139	This is the whole content of the row label, and it is the hinge of §1.2. (The other
   140	`(8,4)` rows of the ROW-SWEEP census fall out of the same formula: `m=7,5,3` give
   141	`β̄_1=9,11,13`, i.e. `gcd(4,β̄_1)=1` and the one-pair germs `(4;9),(4;11),(4;13)`;
   142	`m=2,1` give `β̄_1=14,15`. Every entry of that table is reproduced by `(1.2)`.)
   143	
   144	With `e_0=4`, `e_1=\gcd(4,10)=2`, `e_2=1`, the genus split `δ_aff+δ_∞=p_a=\binom{7}{2}=21`
   145	and `δ_aff=3` give `δ_∞=18`, and the charged cluster identity
   146	`2δ_∞ = (e_0-e_1)β_1+(e_1-e_2)β_2-a+1` reads `36 = 20+β_2-3`, so
   147	
   148	```text
   149	   (a;β_1,β_2) = (4;10,19) ,   β̄ = (4,10,29) ,   Γ_∞ = ⟨4,10,29⟩ .            (1.4)
   150	```
   151	
   152	`β̄_2 = n_1β̄_1+β_2-β_1 = 20+9 = 29`. Sanity: `⟨4,10,29⟩` has gaps `2,6` among the evens
   153	and `1,3,…,27,31,35` among the odds — `2+16 = 18` gaps, so `δ_∞=18` and the conductor is
   154	`36 = 2δ_∞` ✓. Also `M_{emb}=a+β_h-1=4+19-1=22`, `M_∞=\max(8,22)=22`, and `3d-3=21`:
   155	**(M-INF) misses this row by exactly one unit**, which is why it survived.
   156	
   157	Two local facts used later.
   158	
   159	* **(F1′) Maximal smooth contact is 10.** With `u_1:=v` (order 4) and `u_2:=w-v²`
   160	  (order 10), `O_{Q_∞}=C\{u_1,u_2\}` and `\mathrm{ord}(u_1^iu_2^j)=4i+10j`. A smooth germ
   161	  is `ℓ = αv+βw+(\text{ord}≥2) = αu_1+β u_2+βu_1²+\sum_{i+j≥2}c_{ij}u_1^iu_2^j`. If
   162	  `α≠0`, `ord ℓ=4`. If `α=0`, the term `βu_1²` (order 8) can be cancelled only by
   163	  `c_{20}u_1²`, after which every remaining monomial has order `≥12`; and `4i+10j=10`
   164	  forces `(i,j)=(0,1)`, which is not of order `≥2`. Hence `\max = 10`.
   165	  (Contrast the `(6,4)` germ `A_{14}`, where the maximum is `15`.)
   166	* **(F2′) Link type.** Newton pairs `(p_1,q_1)=(2,5)`, `(p_2,q_2)=(2,9)` — the germ is
   167	  the `(2,9)`-cable of the `(2,5)`-torus knot, `μ=2δ_∞=36`.
   168	
   169	### 1.2 Theorem ROW-NF-84: the row is a shear of the (6,4) row
   170	
   171	**Theorem A (ROW-NF-84, PROVED-HERE).** *Let `(P,Q)` be an `(8,4)` row member and set
   172	`R := P-Q²`. Then `deg R = 6`, and `(R,Q)` is a `(6,4)` row member: degrees `(6,4)`,
   173	one place at infinity of multiplicity `2` and `δ=7` (germ `A_{14}`, semigroup `⟨2,15⟩`,
   174	`β_1=15`, `Δ=(6,4,3)`, `M_∞=16`), affine singularities exactly three ordinary nodes.
   175	The shear*
   176	
   177	```text
   178	   Φ : A² → A² ,   Φ(x,y) = (x - y², y)
   179	```
   180	
   181	*is a biregular automorphism of `A²` carrying `D'` onto `D := \mathrm{im}(R,Q)`.
   182	Conversely, for every `(6,4)` row member `(R,Q)` the pair `(R+Q²,Q)` is an `(8,4)` row
   183	member. The two rows are therefore in inverse bijection under `Φ^{±1}`.*
   184	
   185	*Proof.* `deg R = 6` is `(1.3)`. `Φ` is polynomial with polynomial inverse
   186	`(x,y)↦(x+y²,y)`, and `Φ∘(P,Q)=(P-Q²,Q)=(R,Q)`, so `Φ(D')=D` and `Φ` restricts to an
   187	isomorphism of pairs `(A²,D') → (A²,D)`. Hence the affine singularities correspond:
   188	`D` has exactly three ordinary nodes and `δ_aff(D)=3`.
   189	
   190	`(R,Q)` is a polynomial parametrisation, hence has one place at infinity, and it is
   191	birational onto `D` (it is `Φ` composed with a birational map). So `\deg D̄ = \max(6,4)=6`
   192	and `p_a(D̄)=10`, whence `δ_∞(D̄)=10-3=7`. Repeating `(1.1)` for `(R,Q)` gives
   193	`ord v = 2`, `ord w = 6`: the place is unibranch of multiplicity `2`, so its semigroup is
   194	`⟨2,2δ+1⟩=⟨2,15⟩` and the germ is `A_{14}`, i.e. `β_1=15` and `Δ=(6,4,3)` — the sole
   195	survivor of the `(6,4)` row. Conversely, if `(R,Q)` is a `(6,4)` row member then
   196	`P:=R+Q²` has degree `8`, `P-Q²=R` has degree `6`, so `(1.3)` gives `β̄_1=10` and, with
   197	the three nodes preserved, `Δ=(8,4,6,3)` and `(4;10,19)`. `[]`
   198	
   199	**Remark (scope of the equivalence).** Theorem A is *not* the charged "provisional
   200	target-automorphism equivalence" of the ROW-SWEEP witness. ROW-SWEEP exhibited *one*
   201	`(8,4)` curve as `Φ^{-1}` of *one* `(6,4)` curve; Theorem A proves that **every** member
   202	of the `(8,4)` row arises this way, because `deg(P-Q²)=6` *is* the row label `δ_2=6`.
   203	Nothing is inferred by analogy: `(1.2)` is an identity.
   204	
   205	### 1.3 Closed form, modulus, and `δ_aff = 3` by the direct method
   206	
   207	**Lemma FOLD (PROVED-HERE).** *Let `(R,Q)` be a `(6,4)` row member with `R` monic. Then
   208	there are `r ∈ C[t]` monic of degree `3` and constants `μ,κ` with `R = r² + μQ + κ`.*
   209	
   210	*Proof.* The degree semigroup is `Γ=⟨3,4⟩` (§1.1), so some `f∈C[x,y]` has
   211	`\deg_t f(R,Q)=3`; put `r_0:=f(R,Q)`. Then `\deg r_0² = 6 = \deg R`, so after scaling
   212	`r_0` we may assume `\deg(R-r_0²)<6`. Every element of `C[R,Q]` has `t`-degree in `Γ`,
   213	and `Γ∩[0,5]=\{0,3,4\}`. If `\deg(R-r_0²)=4`, subtract `μQ`; the remainder lies in
   214	`Γ∩[0,3]=\{0,3\}`. If it is `3`, it equals `νr_0+(\text{lower})` — replace `r_0` by
   215	`r:=r_0+ν/2`, which changes `r_0²` by `νr_0+ν²/4` and leaves the degree-3 part cancelled.
   216	What remains has `t`-degree in `Γ∩[0,2]=\{0\}`, i.e. is a constant `κ`. `[]`
   217	
   218	Applying the `(6,4)`-preserving target shear `x ↦ x-μy-κ` and the charged ROW-NF
   219	coefficient normalisation (`t`-translation kills `t²` in `r`; `y`-translation kills the
   220	constant of `q`) gives the closed form of the row:
   221	
   222	```text
   223	   D'_j :  x = ρ·r(t)² + q(t)² ,  y = q(t) ,
   224	           r = t³+bt+c ,   q = t⁴+(2b/3)t²+(4c/3)t ,   c ≠ 0 ,  ρ ∈ C^* ,      (1.5)
   225	```
   226	
   227	with modulus `j = b³/c² ∈ C` and three-ordinary-node stratum `j ∉ \{-27/4,-81/16\}`.
   228	The scaling `t↦αt`, `x↦α^{-8}x`, `y↦α^{-4}y` sends `ρ↦ρα^{-2}` and fixes `j`, so `ρ=1`
   229	is a normalisation, at the cost of pinning `(b,c)` within its `j`-orbit. The ROW-SWEEP
   230	witness is `b=c=1`, `ρ=1`, `j=1`.
   231	
   232	**`δ_aff = 3` by the direct method.** Let `Q^{[1]}(t,u) := (Q(t)-Q(u))/(t-u)` and
   233	likewise `P^{[1]}`, `R^{[1]}`. From `P=R+Q²`,
   234	
   235	```text
   236	   P^{[1]} = R^{[1]} + (Q(t)+Q(u))·Q^{[1]} ,
   237	```
   238	
   239	so on `V(Q^{[1]})` one has `P^{[1]} = R^{[1]}`: **the off-diagonal double-point schemes of
   240	`(P,Q)` and `(R,Q)` are the same scheme**, `V(Q^{[1]},R^{[1]})`. This is the divided-
   241	difference form of Theorem A and needs no CAS. For the witness `b=c=1`, in the symmetric
   242	coordinates `σ=t+u`, `π=tu`, that ideal is `(π-σ²-1,\;3σ³+4σ-4)` (charged ROW-SWEEP §2,
   243	re-used, not re-derived): the cubic has nonzero discriminant, its three roots give
   244	pairwise-distinct images and distinct tangent directions, so the scheme is three reduced
   245	unordered pairs. Hence `δ_aff = 3`, realised by three ordinary nodes, and no diagonal or
   246	triple fibre occurs. Together with `p_a=21` this gives `δ_∞=18` and closes `(1.4)`.
