   321	*Proof.* Suppose `P=s²`; `P` is monic, so take `s` monic of degree `4`. Then
   322	`(s-q)(s+q)=P-q²=ρr²` has degree `6` and `\deg(s+q)=4`, so `h:=s-q` has degree exactly
   323	`2` and `h·(h+2q)=ρr²`. On the three-node stratum `\mathrm{disc}(r)=-4b³-27c²=-c²(4j+27)≠0`,
   324	so `r=\prod_{i=1}^3(t-ρ_i)` has distinct roots and `h=\mu h_0` with `h_0` monic dividing `r²`.
   325	
   326	*Case `h_0=(t-ρ_i)(t-ρ_j)`, `i≠j`.* Then
   327	`h+2q = ρr²/h = (ρ/\mu)(t-ρ_i)(t-ρ_j)(t-ρ_k)²`, so
   328	`2q=(t-ρ_i)(t-ρ_j)\big[(ρ/\mu)(t-ρ_k)²-\mu\big]`, i.e. `\deg\gcd(q,r)≥2`. But reducing
   329	`t⁴≡-bt²-ct \bmod r` gives `q ≡ \tfrac{t}{3}(c-bt) \bmod r`, and `r(0)=c≠0`, so
   330	`\gcd(q,r)=\gcd(t(c-bt),r)` has degree `≤1`. Contradiction.
   331	
   332	*Case `h_0=(t-ρ_1)²`.* Then `h+2q=(ρ/\mu)(t-ρ_2)²(t-ρ_3)²`; comparing leading coefficients
   333	gives `ρ/\mu=2`, and comparing coefficients of `t³` (which vanishes in `2q`) gives
   334	`2·(-2)(ρ_2+ρ_3)=0`, i.e. `ρ_2+ρ_3=0`. Since `r` has no `t²` term, `\sum ρ_i=0`, so
   335	`ρ_1=0`; but `r(0)=c≠0`. Contradiction. `[]`
