   337	So the eight points of the fibre `\{x=0\}` are `P(t)=ρr²+q²=0`, which for `ρ=1` factors as
   338	`(q+ir)(q-ir)=0` — **two coprime quartics** (their gcd divides `\gcd(2q,2ir)`, of degree
   339	`≤1`, and a degree-1 common factor would be a common root of `q` and `r`, forcing
   340	`\deg\gcd(q,r)=1` and still leaving eight roots in two groups). Generic fibres carry eight
   341	distinct points, and no identity forces two vertical tangencies into one fibre. The
   342	`(6,4)` degeneration is an artefact of the *sextic* coordinate `p=r²`, which the shear
   343	destroys.
