   175	## 2. Torus-type decision — Oka/Tokunaga conic criterion
   176	
   177	**Definition.** `C` is of *(2,3)-torus type* if `F̄_j = G_2³ + G_3²` for homogeneous
   178	`G_i` of degree `i`. Scaling is free: replacing `(G_2,G_3)` by `(λG_2, μG_3)` with
   179	`λ³=μ²=κ` turns the identity into `κF̄_j`, so "up to `C^*`" costs nothing. Over `C`,
   180	`G_2³ + G_3² = G_2³ − (iG_3)²`, so the `±` convention costs nothing either.
   390	source at all. The `A_1`-vs-`A_{3ι−1}` fact (Lemma 2.2) is common to three of them and
   391	is elementary. I therefore treat Theorem NO-TORUS as settled at the scope of its
   392	hypotheses: `F̄_j` irreducible of degree 6, affine singularities exactly three ordinary
   393	nodes, germ at infinity `A_14`.
   394	
   395	**Scope caveat (recorded, not waived).** Lemma 2.2 fails for germs of type `A_{3ι−1}`.
   396	On the two excluded moduli `j ∈ {−27/4, −81/16}` the affine singular configuration
   397	degenerates (`δ_aff = 3` still, but the type is not `3A_1`); if such a member carried an
   398	affine `A_2` or `A_5`, that point *could* be inner and §2/§3 would not close it. Those
   399	members are outside the campaign's residual, which is the explicit three-node curve.
