   403	## 4. Projective conclusion
   404	
   405	### 4.1 The theorem consumed, verbatim
   406	
   407	Sh12 (fetched and hashed in §0), Corollary 0.6, p. 2:
   408	
   409	> **Corollary 0.6.** Let `Δ` be a divisor of degree 6 on `P²`. Then there is a normal
   410	> triple cover `π` with `Δ_π = Δ` if and only if there are homogeneous polynomials
   411	> `G_i(x_0,x_1,x_2)` of degree `i` for `i = 1,2` [read `i = 2,3`; the displayed identity
   412	> is `G_2³+G_3²`] with the following three conditions:
   413	> (1) `G_2³ + G_3² = 0` defines `Δ`;
   414	> (2) `G_2 ∉ m_E` or `G_3 ∉ m_E²` for any prime divisor `E`; and
   415	> (3) `G_2 ∈ m_E` or `G_2³ + G_3² ∉ m_E²` for any prime divisor `E`.
   416	
   417	Notation, from Sh12 p. 2: a *cover* is a finite flat morphism; a *normal triple cover* is
   418	a degree-3 cover of normal varieties; `Δ_π = S_π + 2T_π` is the **branch divisor**, `π`
   419	being ramified with index 2 along `S_π` and index 3 along `T_π`; `Δ_π := S_π + T_π` is the
   420	reduced branch locus. Remark 0.2 records that a finite surjective morphism from a normal
   421	surface to a smooth surface is automatically a normal cover — so no flatness hypothesis
   422	has to be checked by hand.
   423	
   424	**Why this and not R10 (IT09 Thm 1.1).** Sh12 Cor 0.6 is strictly stronger for my
   425	purposes on three counts: it needs **no simple-singularity hypothesis**, it needs **no
   426	genericity hypothesis** (no "finitely many total branch points"), and it is an **iff for
   427	arbitrary normal triple covers**, not only non-Galois generic ones. Sh12 Rem 0.7(iv)
   428	states explicitly that it generalises the Ishida–Tokunaga result. Escape (b) of the
   429	charge — "the extension is not generic in R10's sense" — is therefore closed by
   430	*replacing the theorem*, not by verifying genericity; see §6.
   431	
   432	### 4.2 What dies
   433	
   434	**Proposition 4.1 (PROVED-HERE).** *Let `ψ : π₁(C²−D_j) → S_3` be any homomorphism with
   435	all meridians of `D_j` mapping to transpositions and with `ψ` surjective, and suppose
   436	`ψ(γ_∞) = 1` for a meridian `γ_∞` of `L_∞`. Then a contradiction follows.*
