   489	`Z/3` is abelian, so `ψ(γ_∞)` is a **well-defined element**, independent of the choice of
   490	meridian (all meridians of `L_∞` are conjugate). Abelianisation gives nothing further: the
   491	dichotomy `ψ(γ_∞) ∈ {e, 3-cycle}` is genuine at this level. This is exactly the charge's
   492	"is the `L_∞` meridian image trivial or a 3-cycle" question.
   493	
   494	### 5.2 The three identifications at `x = 0`
   495	
   496	Project along `x`. `D_j → C_x` is a 6-section (`r(t)² = x` has six roots). Over `x = 0`
   497	the six points collide **in three disjoint pairs**: `r(t)² = 0 ⟺ r(t) = 0`, whose roots
   498	`τ_1,τ_2,τ_3` are simple and distinct on the stratum `j ≠ −27/4`. Near `x=0`,
   499	`r(t) = ±√x` gives `t = τ_i ± √x/r'(τ_i) + O(x)` and
   500	
   501	```text
   502	   y = q(τ_i) ± ( q'(τ_i)/r'(τ_i) ) √x + O(x) ,   i = 1,2,3 ,                   (5.2)
   503	```
   504	
   505	with `q'(τ_i) ≠ 0` (`q' ≡ −(8/3)(bt+c) mod r`, and `bt+c` shares no root with `r` unless
   506	`c=0`, excluded). So each pair is a two-valued branch swapping under `x ↦ e^{2πi}x`: the
   507	local braid at `x=0` is a product of **three simultaneous, pairwise disjoint half-twists**.
   508	In a geometric basis of the base fibre `{x = x_*}` (`|x_*|` small) adapted to the three
   509	clusters — indices `{1,2}`, `{3,4}`, `{5,6}` in cluster order — this is
   510	`β_0 = σ_1σ_3σ_5`. This is the charged `zvk-u6` §3.2 datum, re-derived here.
   511	
   512	The Zariski–van Kampen relation `β_0·T = T` for a half-twist on adjacent strands is the
   513	**identification** of the two meridians, so in `G := π₁(C²−D_j)`:
   514	
   515	```text
   516	   g_1 = g_2 ,   g_3 = g_4 ,   g_5 = g_6 .                                      (5.3)
   517	```
   518	
   519	*Exponent cross-check.* `e(ρ_∞) = 3·1 (the three σ's at x=0) + 2·1 (the two simple
   520	vertical tangencies at the roots of r′) + 3·2 (the three nodes) = 11`, matching the
   521	independent Puiseux computation `16 − 5 = 11` of charged `zvk-u6` §2.4. The three
   522	half-twists at `x=0` are counted once each, so `(5.3)` is three relations, not six.
