   290	For a geometric basis, a meridian $\gamma_\infty$ of the line at infinity is the
   291	ordered boundary product of the $g_i$ (up to the inverse/conjugacy dictated by
   292	the Artin convention).  If $\tau_i=\rho(g_i)$, then
   293	
   294	\[
   295	 \rho(\gamma_\infty)\sim \tau_1\cdots\tau_d=:\Pi,
   296	 \qquad \operatorname{sgn}\rho(\gamma_\infty)=(-1)^d.             \tag{4.2}
   297	\]
   298	
   299	Only triviality and sign will be used, so the harmless convention in (4.2) is
   300	immaterial.
   301	
   302	### 4.1 Coprime infinity
   303	
   304	If $g=1$, the roots over a large circle satisfy uniformly
   305	$t_j\sim cR^{1/d}e^{i(\theta+2\pi j)/d}$ and their $q$-values have leading
   306	phase $n(\theta+2\pi j)/d$.  Coprimality keeps the leading points separated,
   307	so the error is isotopic away in configuration space.  Thus, up to conjugacy,
   308	
   309	\[
   310	 \beta_\infty=\delta_d^n,\qquad
   311	 \delta_d=\sigma_1\cdots\sigma_{d-1}.                             \tag{4.3}
   312	\]
   313	
   314	Here
   315	
   316	\[
   317	 \delta_\infty=\frac{(d-n-1)(d-1)}2,\quad
   318	 \delta_{\rm aff}=\frac{(n-1)(d-1)}2,\quad
   319	 M_\infty=2d-n-1,                                                  \tag{4.4}
   320	\]
   321	
   322	and (4.1) becomes $e(\beta_\infty)=n(d-1)$, checking (4.3).
   323	
   324	### 4.2 Cable infinity and the INF-TRIVIAL stop
   325	
   326	If (g\geq2), put (d'=d/g) and (n'=n/g).  The leading motion has (d') outer
   327	clusters of (g) strands and outer torus braid (\delta_{d'}^{n'}), but the
   328	inner braid and its iterated cabling depend on the missing later entries of the
   329	delta-sequence.  The underlying full permutation and (4.1) are known; a word in
   330	(B_d) is not.
   331	
   332	Blackboard (g)-cabling multiplies every outer crossing into (g^2) crossings, so
   333	the outer contribution has exponent (n(d-g)).  If (\iota) denotes all inner
   334	levels, the exact remaining exponent is
   335	
   336	\[
   337	             e(\iota)=d-1+2\delta_{\rm aff}-n(d-g).                 \tag{4.5}
   338	\]
   339	
   340	This integer, which may be negative, is only an exponent sum and does not recover
   341	the inner word.  For the conditional old row ((d,n),\delta_{\rm aff})=((6,4),3),
   342	it is (-5), while the full exponent is (11); those numbers do not constitute a
   343	ROW-NF for S2.
   344	
   345	The N=4 INF-TRIVIAL proof used an exact fold fibre whose disjoint half-twists
   346	identified adjacent meridians, turning (\gamma_\infty) into a product of squares.
   347	That implication is target-independent: once such a relation is proved, applying
   348	(\rho) itself kills every square.  S2, however, supplies neither a fold equation
   349	nor a paired collision fibre.  A product of an even number of transpositions can
   350	be a nonidentity even permutation.  Therefore
   351	
   352	```text
   353	OPEN[N5-S2-INF-TRIVIAL-CABLE]:
   354	  from the full S2 Puiseux/delta-sequence and an admissible projection, decide
   355	  the boundary product in the cable-fixed transposition tuple; sign is insufficient.
   356	```
   357	
   358	For odd (d), (4.2) already says full INF-TRIVIAL is impossible.  For even (d), it
   359	remains undecided until the exact cable or an equivalent local-link/ZvK relation
   360	is supplied.
   361	
   362	## 5. ROW-KILL / Theorem A test
   363	
   364	### 5.1 Coprime S2 is killed
   365	
   366	Assume (g=1) and let (T=(\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_d)) be the transposition tuple in a
   367	geometric fibre, with (\Pi=\tau_1\cdots\tau_d).  The Hurwitz action satisfies
   368	
   369	\[
   370	 \delta_d(T)=(\Pi\tau_d\Pi^{-1},\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_{d-1}),
   371	\]
   372	
   373	and (T) is fixed by (\delta_d^n), from (4.3).  Since (\delta_d^d) conjugates
   374	every entry by (\Pi), fixedness under (\delta_d^{nd}) says that (\Pi^n)
   375	centralizes the generated (S_5).  Its centre is trivial, so
   376	
   377	\[
   378	                             \Pi^n=1.                              \tag{5.1}
   379	\]
   380	
   381	Extend the entries periodically as in the standard Theorem A proof.  Coprimality
   382	of (d,n) makes all entries one orbit under conjugation by (\Pi).  Such an orbit
   383	of transpositions generates (S_5) exactly when its edge orbit is connected on
   384	five vertices.  Enumerating the seven cycle types in (S_5) leaves only
   385	
   386	| cycle type of (\Pi) | connected edge orbit | forced arithmetic |
   387	|---|---|---|
   388	| ((3)(2)) | all six edges of (K_{3,2}) | (6\mid n), (d) odd |
   389	| ((4)(1)) | the four-edge star from the fixed letter | (4\mid n), (d) odd |
   390	| ((5)) | a five-cycle (or its diagonal cycle) | (5\mid n), (d) even |
   391	
   392	Identity, (2), (2^2), and ((3)(1^2)) have no edge orbit meeting all five
   393	vertices.  The last column uses (5.1) and
   394	(\operatorname{sgn}\Pi=(-1)^d).  In particular, every hypothetical coprime
   395	representation has (n\geq4).
   396	
   397	Now vary (p,q) in the irreducible coefficient space of pairs of degrees ((d,n)).
   398	Because (\gcd(d,n)=1), every pair is birational onto its image, its infinity
   399	germ and (\delta_\infty) stay fixed, and a generic nearby pair is immersive with
   400	only ordinary nodes (the bounds (d>n\geq4) provide jet separation).  A contact
   401	(A_{2k-1}) of the special curve splits into (k) nearby nodes.  On complement
   402	groups, passage from the special curve to that nearby nodal curve adds the
   403	commutators of the two branch meridians.  Their images under (\rho) are disjoint
   404	transpositions, hence commute, so (\rho) factors through the nearby nodal
   405	complement.
   406	
   407	For that nodal curve, (T=0) and (4.4) gives
   408	
   409	\[
   410	             M_\infty=2d-n-1\leq2d-2\leq3d-3.
   411	\]
   412	
   413	Promoted N-A-RES (`CNA:44-49`) therefore makes its complement group (\mathbb Z),
   414	which cannot surject onto (S_5).  This contradiction proves the new, review-gated
   415	result
   416	
   417	```text
   418	THEOREM[S5-COPRIME-KILL]: no S2 representation exists when gcd(d,n)=1.
   419	```
   420	
   421	This fills B0's order-3/order-4 transfer gap by doing the (S_5) orbit enumeration,
   422	not by reusing the (S_4) conclusion.  It needs neither a resolvent nor
   423	INF-TRIVIAL.
   424	
   425	Conditional row consequence: the two provisional triangular reductions at
   426	`CNA:76-77`, from the labelled ((6,3)-(7,4)) and ((6,3)-(8,3)) rows to
   427	coprime ((5,3)) and ((4,3)), now have an (S_5) terminal kill by the theorem
   428	above.  This transfers those reductions at their existing row-geometry scope;
   429	it does not assert that either row is pinned by S2.
   430	
   431	### 5.2 One cable row also dies: normalized degrees (6,4)
   432	
   433	This row is not pinned by S2, but it is the closest N=4-shaped cable and can be
   434	decided without its delta-sequence.  Its six strands form three leading pairs;
   435	the outer braid is (\delta_3^2).  In a cable-adapted tuple put
   436	
   437	\[
   438	 A=\tau_1\tau_2,\quad B=\tau_3\tau_4,\quad C=\tau_5\tau_6,\quad P=ABC.
   439	\]
   440	
   441	Inner two-strand braids preserve each block product.  Applying block products to
   442	full fixedness therefore gives (\delta_3^2(A,B,C)=(A,B,C)).  Direct Hurwitz
   443	calculation yields
   444	
   445	\[
   446	 C=A,\qquad ABA=BAB.                                               \tag{5.2}
   447	\]
   448	
   449	The conjugate elements (A,B) are products of two transpositions, hence are both
   450	identity, both 3-cycles, or both double transpositions.
   451	
   452	* If they are identity, the six entries use at most three edges, too few to
   453	  connect five vertices.
   454	* Two 3-cycle supports must intersect.  If their union had five letters, their
   455	  supports would meet once; conjugate to (A=(123)) and
   456	  (B=(145)) or ((154)), direct evaluation violates (5.2).  Thus all factors fix
   457	  a fifth letter.
   458	* For double transpositions, (5.2) is equivalent to ((AB)^3=1).  If (A=B), their
   459	  support has four letters.  If distinct, view them as two two-edge matchings:
   460	  product order three occurs exactly when they share one edge (no shared edge
   461	  gives order two with the same fixed vertex, or order five with different fixed
   462	  vertices).  Their unique disjoint-edge factorizations then preserve the shared
   463	  2-set and its complementary 3-set.
   464	
   465	Every case gives an intransitive factor graph, contrary to generation of (S_5).
   466	Thus the whole ((6,4)) cable is killed, including any member with the old
   467	(\Delta=(6,4,3)) data.  No conclusion is drawn for an arbitrary noncoprime pair:
   468	other outer block lengths and the missing inner cables require their own
   469	block-product analysis.
   470	
   471	## 6. Verdict and first missing tool
   472	
   473	**Verdict: S2 remains OPEN, but its entire coprime stratum is KILLED and its
   474	normalized (6,4) cable stratum is KILLED.**  The same conclusion applies to both
   475	P3a and P3b; their extra components have trivial meridians.  The surviving question
   476	is now
   477	
   478	```text
   479	OPEN[PI1-S5-NODAL-NONCOPRIME]:
   480	  Does there exist an irreducible polynomial curve D, normalization A1 and one
   481	  place at infinity, with a degree-reduced parametrization (p,q), d>n>=1,
   482	  g=gcd(d,n)>=2, every affine singularity two smooth branches of contact k_p,
   483	  M_infinity+2T >= 3d-2, and a surjection pi1(C2-D)->S5 sending meridians to
   484	  transpositions and each local branch pair to disjoint transpositions?
   485	  Exclude the already killed normalized pair (d,n)=(6,4).
   486	```
   487	
   488	This is a necessary cage, not a realization statement.  It is strictly narrower
   489	than B0's DQ-1 because §5.1 removes (g=1), but it is not one numerical row.
   490	
   491	The **first missing tool is geometric**, before a quintic-cover classification:
   492	
   493	```text
   494	OPEN[N5-S2-ROW-PIN]:
   495	  derive from the reviewed N=5 Keller data either an exact degree/delta-sequence
   496	  list for D (including all approximate-root remainders and the infinity
   497	  multiplicity sequence), or a degree cap that makes that list finite.
   498	```
   499	
   500	Without ROW-PIN there is no S2 infinity type on which to run a full cable braid,
   501	no licensed fold relation, and no value of (\rho(\gamma_\infty)) to feed the
   502	projective-cover route.  Once a row is pinned, the next decision is explicit:
   503	
   504	1. compute its iterated-cable braid and block-product action and decide the fixed
   505	   five-letter transposition tuple (the §5.2 calculation is the model);
   506	2. if that calculation proves full INF-TRIVIAL rather than killing the tuple,
   507	   invoke `OPEN[N5-S2-QUINTIC-PLANE]` from §2, not the S4 triple-plane theorem.
   508	
   509	The classical sextic resolvent does not change this order: it retains full (S_5),
   510	turns each simple inertia into (2^3), and supplies no classified triple plane.
   511	The sign quotient supplies only parity.  Thus no safe replacement presently kills
   512	all noncoprime S2 rows, and the fail-closed status is OPEN.
   513	
