xmodel/b0-pi1-acquisition-grok46-20260831.md:78:Zariski’s flex theorem (Oka ICTP (I) and statement (3); Cogolludo, *Braid monodromy of algebraic curves*, Thm. 2.13, hashed Numdam AMBP 2011): if an irreducible curve of degree `d` has a line `L` with `L ∩ C = {Q}` and `Q` a *smooth* point (flex of order `d−2`, i.e. `I(C,L;Q) = d`), then `π₁(C²_L − C)` is abelian — the local monodromy is `G(d,1) ≅ Z`, all meridians of a generic fibre become equal, so the group is cyclic. Cogolludo’s projective Thm. 2.13 drops smoothness of `Q` but concludes only `π₁(P² − C)` abelian, which is the wrong group (see Oka Remark 3). Oka Prop. 23: a *single* unibranch singularity with a maximal tangent also makes both groups abelian; our `D` may have additional affine double points, so Prop. 23 does not apply.
